From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:06:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2764: Bring an SDZ group back from a deep
 sleep as from power-on

Amps in an SDZ group keep the shared SDZ line across system sleep and
only sync their register cache at resume. That holds for s2idle. A
firmware-driven sleep powers the SoC's pin controller off, so the line
drops while asleep and every amp comes back from a power-on reset when
the pin controller restores it: TI's volatile quirk page is gone, and
the page register is back at 0 while the cache may still hold another
page. On the J604 after a deep-sleep wake, all six amps then fail their
writes at the first stream (ASoC errors -6 and -5 on every amp).

On a resume via firmware, treat a group like a power-on: wait the SDZ
to I2C time, reset the amp, drop the cached page so the next page
switch reads the amp, sync the cache and apply the init quirks again,
as the probe does. Say so when the amp does not answer on I2C, which
means SDZ or the supply did not come back. s2idle keeps today's path.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
index 403b98f676..82a90810d9 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <linux/refcount.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
@@ -434,6 +435,14 @@ static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 {
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
+	/*
+	 * A firmware-driven (deep) sleep powers the SoC's pin controller off,
+	 * so the group's SDZ line drops while asleep and every amp comes back
+	 * from a power-on reset when the pin controller restores the line
+	 * (its resume_noirq, before this). s2idle keeps the line and the
+	 * amps' registers and stays on the path below.
+	 */
+	bool power_on = tas2764->sdz_group && pm_resume_via_firmware();
 	int ret;
 
 	if (!tas2764->sdz_group) {
@@ -451,6 +460,25 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, false);
 
+	if (power_on) {
+		/* SDZ high to the first I2C access, as at probe */
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+		/*
+		 * Start from the reset state the cache sync assumes. The page
+		 * register is back at 0 while the cache may hold another page:
+		 * drop it, so the next page switch is read from the amp.
+		 */
+		ret = regmap_write(tas2764->regmap, TAS2764_SW_RST, TAS2764_RST);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(tas2764->dev,
+				"no answer on I2C after a deep sleep (%d): SDZ or supply not back\n",
+				ret);
+			goto out_irq;
+		}
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+		regcache_drop_region(tas2764->regmap, TAS2764_PAGE, TAS2764_PAGE);
+	}
+
 	ret = regcache_sync(tas2764->regmap);
 	/*
 	 * An amp whose SDZ went low came back from a power-on reset, and the
@@ -460,8 +488,11 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	 * over-current at the first stream after a resume. Apply them again,
 	 * as the probe does.
 	 */
-	if (ret >= 0 && !tas2764->sdz_group && tas2764->devid == DEVID_SN012776)
+	if (ret >= 0 && (!tas2764->sdz_group || power_on) &&
+	    tas2764->devid == DEVID_SN012776)
 		ret = tas2764_apply_init_quirks(tas2764);
+	if (ret < 0 && power_on)
+		dev_err(tas2764->dev, "restore after a deep sleep failed: %d\n", ret);
 	if (ret >= 0)
 		ret = tas2764_update_pwr_ctrl(tas2764);
 
-- 
2.43.0

